add('Prime-stripped factorials have an exact recursive block algorithm','strip powers','2026-10-09','A','p-adic arithmetic',
'How can the unit part of n! modulo pᵏ be computed without ever dividing a nonunit modulo pᵏ?',
'The recovered factorial-unit claim becomes a clean integer recursion. Removing powers of p must occur before modular reduction; division by p after reduction is not legitimate. The same decomposition yields a logarithmic-depth algorithm with exact residue-block products.',
['For prime p, let v_p(n!)=Σ_(j≥1)⌊n/pʲ⌋ and U_p(n)=n!/p^(v_p(n!)), with U_p(0)=1.','Set m=pᵏ, W=∏_(1≤i≤m,p∤i)i modulo m, and V(r)=∏_(1≤i≤r,p∤i)i modulo m for 0≤r<m.'],
'Unit recursion and complete-block reduction',
'U_p(n)=[∏_(1≤i≤n,p∤i)i]U_p(⌊n/p⌋) as integers. Consequently U_p(n)≡∏_(j≥0)W^(⌊⌊n/pʲ⌋/m⌋)V(⌊n/pʲ⌋ mod m) modulo m, with only finitely many nontrivial factors.',
'Partition the factors of n! into indices not divisible by p and indices i=pj. For the latter, stripping all powers of p leaves j/p^(v_p(j)), precisely the factors defining U_p(⌊n/p⌋). This proves the integer recursion, rather than an invalid modular cancellation. To compute its first factor modulo m, write n=am+r. Each complete interval ℓm+1,…,(ℓ+1)m has the same reduced residues modulo m; because p divides m, it also has exactly the same set of positions not divisible by p. Its unit product is therefore W. The remaining r positions contribute V(r). Iterating with n replaced by its successive p-quotients terminates at zero. The valuation formula follows separately by counting each factor p once for multiples of p, again for multiples of p², and so on. Both claims are finite integer factorizations and need no limiting p-adic argument.',
'Compute the unit part of 10! modulo nine.',
'For p=3, v₃(10!)=3+1=4, so U₃(10)=3628800/81=44800≡7 mod 9. The nonmultiples-of-three product through ten is 22400≡8 mod 9, while U₃(3)=2. Their product gives 8·2≡7 mod 9, independently matching the stripped factorial.',
'The recursion preserves every unit factor and avoids modular division by three.',
'A residue of the unit part is not the factorial itself; the valuation must be retained. Cancellation in a factorial ratio requires combining valuations and inverting only residues coprime to p. Historical sequence-family checks are not replayed merely by proving this recursion.',
'Apply the method to ∏(a_j n+b_j)!^(e_j): compute its exact valuation first, then its unit ratio modulo pᵏ, and state explicitly whether a negative valuation gives a p-adic number rather than an integer.',
[('NIST DLMF: arithmetic functions','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.2')])
add('Perfect-power images admit complete valuation parametrizations','2x^2=3y^3','2026-10-05','A','Diophantine parametrization',
'Can the nonlinear integer image be parametrized in both directions, including signs and zero?',
'The recovered power-chart examples admit global elementary proofs. Prime valuations establish surjectivity of the parametrization; substitution alone would show only that a proposed family gives some solutions. Keeping the reverse direction is essential for exact enumeration or inverse lookup.',
['For a nonzero integer a and a prime p, v_p(a) is the exponent of p in |a|.','All variables in the following statements are integers; powers and signs are interpreted in Z, not merely over positive reals.'],
'Complete cusp parametrizations',
'x²=y³ if and only if (x,y)=(t³,t²) for a unique t∈Z. Also 2x²=3y³ if and only if (x,y)=(18t³,6t²) for a unique t∈Z.',
'The zero case forces both coordinates to vanish. Otherwise y>0. In x²=y³, the equality 2v_p(x)=3v_p(y) implies v_p(x)=3a_p and v_p(y)=2a_p for nonnegative integers a_p. Thus y=t² and |x|=|t|³; choose the sign of t to match x. This t is unique because integer cubing is injective. For 2x²=3y³, at p=2 we have 1+2a=3b, hence b=2r+1 and a=3r+1 with r≥0. At p=3, 2a=1+3b gives b=2s+1 and a=3s+2. Every other prime again has valuations (3a_p,2a_p). Extracting these factors yields |x|=18u³ and y=6u², where u≥1 is an integer. The sign of x determines t=±u. Conversely, direct substitution gives 2·18²t⁶=3·6³t⁶, so the parametrization is exact.',
'Invert a concrete point and impose an arithmetic restriction.',
'For (x,y)=(−144,24), x/18=−8 gives t=−2, and 6t²=24 checks the other coordinate. Restricting y≤150 gives |t|≤5, hence exactly eleven solutions including zero. A further congruence can be tested on the single parameter; for example x≡0 mod 5 is equivalent to t≡0 mod 5 because 18 is invertible modulo five.',
'The nonlinear image becomes an explicitly invertible one-parameter integer population.',
'Adding a nonzero constant generally destroys this cusp parametrization: y²=x³+k is an elliptic curve when k≠0 and its discriminant is nonzero. No global Mordell completeness theorem follows from the singular k=0 case.',
'For a x^m=b y^n, separate primes dividing ab and use gcd(m,n) to solve each valuation congruence; assemble finitely many coefficient classes and prove their images disjoint before supplying rank/unrank algorithms.',
[('NIST DLMF: unique factorization and arithmetic functions','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.2')])
add('Integer lifting turns monomial equations into finite exponent lattices','exactly 12 positive','2026-10-04','A','Integer lattices',
'How can linear Diophantine lifting and prime-exponent constraints yield a complete, exactly countable population?',
'The retrieved examples illustrate two forms of integer lifting: an affine line of solutions and a finite product of exponent lattices. Both require divisibility conditions, rather than a real-valued inverse followed by rounding. The exponent formulation is also the correct setting for Smith normal form compatibility tests.',
['A positive integer has a unique vector of nonnegative prime exponents.','A unimodular integer change of variables has determinant ±1 and preserves the integer lattice; Smith normal form diagonalizes an integer linear map using such changes.'],
'Complete affine and multiplicative solution sets',
'All integer solutions of 2x+3y=1 are (x,y)=(−1+3t,1−2t), t∈Z. The equation x²y³=2²⁰3¹² has exactly twelve positive integer solutions, indexed by 0≤r≤3 and 0≤s≤2 as x=2^(10−3r)3^(6−3s), y=2^(2r)3^(2s).',
'Reducing 2x+3y=1 modulo three gives x≡−1 mod 3, so x=−1+3t; substitution forces y=1−2t. Every t works, proving completeness and injectivity. For the multiplicative equation, no prime other than two or three can divide x or y, because all exponents are nonnegative. Write x=2ᵃ3ᶜ and y=2ᵇ3ᵈ. Unique factorization gives 2a+3b=20 and 2c+3d=12. The first equation forces b even, b=2r, and then a=10−3r≥0, so r=0,1,2,3. The second similarly gives d=2s and c=6−3s≥0, so s=0,1,2. These choices are independent and unique, producing 4·3=12 solutions. More generally, if UAV=diag(d₁,…,d_r,0,…) is a Smith decomposition of an exponent map, integer solvability requires d_i to divide (Ub)_i and zero coordinates of Ub to vanish; nonnegativity is an additional constraint after applying V.',
'Enumerate two endpoints and construct a reversible address.',
'At (r,s)=(0,0), (x,y)=(2¹⁰3⁶,1); at (3,2), (x,y)=(2,2⁶3⁴). The address j=3r+s runs from zero to eleven; division with remainder recovers r=⌊j/3⌋ and s=j mod 3. Thus the count and inverse lookup are both exact.',
'Prime-exponent reduction proves every listed point and excludes every omitted positive point.',
'Negative or zero solutions need separate sign and support cases. Smith invariant factors alone do not enforce nonnegativity, and a claimed perfect-cube compatibility obstruction requires the actual lattice map, not just a numerical determinant.',
'Combine Smith coordinates with polyhedral lattice-point enumeration to count bounded exponent populations and optimize a stated linear cost in the original exponents, preserving a certificate of every divisibility and sign condition.',
[('Stanley: Smith normal form in combinatorics','https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.00166')])
add('The negative Pell equation splits into two complete unit orbits','(x^2+7)/2','2026-10-08','A','Pell equations',
'Can the integer solutions of x²+7=2y² be organized into complete transport orbits rather than generated only experimentally?',
'The recovered quadratic equation is a negative Pell equation. Multiplication by the fundamental norm-one unit gives exact transport, and reduction to a compact interval produces a finite seed proof. This supplies the global completeness argument missing from a table of recurrent solutions.',
['In Z[√2], N(x+y√2)=x²−2y². Let ε=3+2√2, with N(ε)=1 and ε⁻¹=3−2√2.','For y>0 and N(α)=−7, the real number α=x+y√2 is positive because |x|<√2y.'],
'Two-orbit completeness',
'Every solution with y>0 is given uniquely by α=(1+2√2)εⁿ or α=(5+4√2)εⁿ for n∈Z. Solutions with y<0 are obtained by negation; y=0 gives none.',
'Multiplying by ε sends (x,y) to (3x+4y,2x+3y), an invertible integer map preserving norm. For any positive α of norm −7 there is a unique n such that β=αε^(−n) lies in [√7,ε√7). Its conjugate is −7/β, so its coordinates satisfy x_β=(β−7/β)/2≥0 and y_β=(β+7/β)/(2√2). On this interval y_β≥√(7/2) and y_β<3√(7/2)<6, hence y_β is one of 2,3,4,5. The equation x_β²=2y_β²−7 gives respectively 1,11,25,43; only y_β=2,4 yield squares. Thus β is exactly 1+2√2 or 5+4√2. Both lie in the reduction interval, and the half-open interval gives uniqueness of the exponent and seed. Conversely, multiplying either seed by any integer power of ε preserves integral coordinates, positive α and norm −7; then y=(α+7/α)/(2√2)>0. This proves both coverage and absence of duplicates.',
'Generate and reverse a transported solution.',
'The first seed (1,2) maps to (11,8), since 3+8=11 and 2+6=8; indeed 121+7=2·64. The second seed (5,4) maps to (31,22). Applying ε⁻¹ sends (11,8) to (3·11−4·8,−2·11+3·8)=(1,2).',
'Two seeds and an invertible recurrence describe the entire integer solution set, with the sign cases explicit.',
'A norm-preserving recurrence by itself proves only closure. Completeness here depends on the compact reduction and exhaustive seed analysis. Different quadratic orders, norms and unit groups require their own reduction argument.',
'Intersect the two orbits with modular conditions by computing the unit action on (Z/mZ)²; use the resulting finite cycles to obtain exact admissible exponent classes and bounded rank/select operations.',
[('Lenstra: solving the Pell equation','https://www.ams.org/notices/200202/fea-lenstra.pdf')])
add('GCD matrices factor through divisor incidence and discrete Green kernels','Divisor sums diagonalize','2026-10-04','A','Arithmetic linear algebra',
'Which exact incidence factorization explains invertibility, determinant and solution of a GCD linear system?',
'The recovered GCD-kernel discussion has a precise arithmetic factorization. A related cumulative-sum kernel explains why a dense covariance matrix can have an integer tridiagonal inverse. These are separate examples of a common incidence-matrix principle.',
['Let G_n have entries gcd(i,j), 1≤i,j≤n, and E_ij=1 when j divides i, zero otherwise. Let φ be Euler’s totient.','For the second example, let L_ij=1 when j≤i and K_ij=min(i,j).'],
'Exact arithmetic and chain-incidence factorizations',
'G_n=E diag(φ(1),…,φ(n))Eᵀ, so det G_n=∏_(j=1)^nφ(j)>0. Also K=LLᵀ and K⁻¹=DᵀD, where D has diagonal one and subdiagonal −1. Its diagonal is (2,…,2,1) and its adjacent off-diagonals are −1.',
'The divisor identity Σ_(d|m)φ(d)=m follows by grouping the integers 1,…,m according to their gcd with m. Hence (E diagφ Eᵀ)_ij=Σ_(d|i,d|j)φ(d)=gcd(i,j). E is lower triangular with diagonal one, proving the determinant and positive definiteness. Solving Gx=b reduces to two triangular substitutions and positive scalar divisions; the inverse incidence matrix is the divisibility Möbius matrix, by the identity Σ_(d|m)μ(d)=0 for m>1. For the chain kernel, (LLᵀ)_ij counts indices at most both i and j, giving min(i,j). D=L⁻¹ because first differences invert cumulative sums. Therefore K⁻¹=DᵀD; direct multiplication gives the stated tridiagonal entries. The last diagonal is one because the final coordinate occurs in only the last row of D.',
'Check the n=3 chain kernel and a GCD determinant.',
'K=[[1,1,1],[1,2,2],[1,2,3]] has inverse [[2,−1,0],[−1,2,−1],[0,−1,1]]. The GCD matrix [[1,1,1],[1,2,1],[1,1,3]] has determinant φ(1)φ(2)φ(3)=2. Both identities are verified by exact matrix multiplication.',
'Dense kernels can have sparse inverses because they encode an integrated local difference operator.',
'The min kernel is a newly chosen chain model, not an identification of every unspecified normalized GCD kernel in the source. A factorization over Q does not imply its inverse has integer entries; the totient diagonal generally introduces denominators.',
'Replace gcd(i,j) by f(gcd(i,j)); Möbius inversion gives diagonal weights (f*μ)(d). Positivity of those weights is then an explicit sufficient condition for positive definiteness on the initial divisor-closed set.',
[('NIST DLMF: divisor sums and Möbius inversion','https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.6')])
add('Superelliptic genus follows from finite and infinite ramification','Superelliptic geometry','2026-10-04','A','Algebraic curves',
'Under exactly which assumptions do the recovered genus, curvature and local monodromy formulas hold?',
'The source gives a superelliptic genus formula and its Gauss–Bonnet counterpart. Both become rigorous on the smooth compact normalization of a squarefree equation. The point at infinity must be included: overlooking its gcd contribution changes the genus.',
['Let P∈C[x] be squarefree of degree m≥1, let d≥2, and let C be the smooth projective normalization of yᵈ=P(x). Set δ=gcd(d,m).','The map x:C→P¹ has degree d. Curvature below is the Gaussian curvature of any smooth Riemannian metric on the compact oriented surface C.'],
'Genus, curvature and branch monodromy',
'g(C)=[(d−1)(m−1)−δ+1]/2 and ∫_C K dA=2π[d+δ−m(d−1)]. Around a simple zero of P, analytic continuation multiplies a local branch of y by exp(2πi/d).',
'Near a simple zero a, write P(x)=(x−a)u(x), u(a)≠0. A holomorphic dth root of u exists locally, so a local parameter t can be chosen with x−a=tᵈ. The ramification index is d, contributing d−1 at each of the m finite branch points. At infinity the equation has orders d·ord(y)=m·ord(x); dividing by δ shows that there are δ points above infinity, each with ramification index d/δ. Their total contribution is d−δ. Riemann–Hurwitz gives 2g−2=−2d+m(d−1)+(d−δ)=m(d−1)−d−δ, which rearranges to the stated formula. Gauss–Bonnet gives ∫K=2π(2−2g), yielding the curvature expression. Finally, moving x−a once counterclockwise adds 2π to its argument, so its dth root and therefore y acquire exp(2πi/d). This is local covering monodromy, distinct from an arbitrary flat-connection holonomy.',
'Compare an elliptic and a higher-genus example.',
'For y²=x³−x, d=2,m=3,δ=1 gives g=1 and total curvature zero. For y³=x⁴−1, d=3,m=4,δ=1 gives g=3 and total curvature −8π. In the latter, every finite root has local monodromy exp(2πi/3), while infinity is also totally ramified.',
'The recovered formulas hold after the singular affine equation is replaced by its smooth compact curve.',
'Repeated roots require revised local ramification data and can make the displayed genus formula false. Gauss–Bonnet controls total curvature, not a pointwise curvature sign, and an affine noncompact chart has boundary/end corrections.',
'For yᵈ=∏(x−a_j)^(r_j), derive ramification contributions d−gcd(d,r_j), include infinity, and separate connected components when the equation is a nontrivial common power.',
[('Stacks Project: Riemann–Hurwitz','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C1B')])
add('Cubic norms certify unit transport but not an unreplayed global search','norm \\(\\pm107','2026-10-03','T','Algebraic number theory',
'Which exact cubic identities can be checked directly, and what is still required to prove the claimed global uniqueness of a norm-equation solution?',
'The retrieved monograph claims a complete solution of u³+4v³=107 and records two units. The available evidence does not include its slab bounds or unit-exponent exclusion certificate. We can nevertheless derive the exact norm form, replay the units and the displayed solution, and state the missing completeness obligation without substituting a finite search for it.',
['Let z³=−4 and use the integral basis 1,z,z² of the order Z[z]. Define N(a+bz+cz²) as the determinant of multiplication on this free Z-module.','An element with norm ±1 is a unit of this order because its integer multiplication matrix has an integer inverse.'],
'Exact cubic norm and unit identities',
'N(a+bz+cz²)=a³−4b³+16c³+12abc. The elements η=5−3z+2z² and ε=1−z−z² both have norm one. Also N(u−vz)=u³+4v³, and (u,v)=(−1,3) gives norm 107.',
'Multiplication by a+bz+cz² in the indicated basis has matrix [[a,−4c,−4b],[b,a,−4c],[c,b,a]]. Expanding its determinant gives the stated cubic form, proving the identity without numeric algebraic approximations. For η, the four contributions are 125,108,128,−360, summing to one. For ε they are 1,4,−16,12, again summing to one. Determinants multiply, so multiplication by any ηʳεˢ preserves a norm equation. Setting (a,b,c)=(u,−v,0) gives u³+4v³; the displayed point gives −1+108=107. These calculations prove valid transport in a three-coordinate norm surface. They do not show that unit multiplication keeps c=0, nor that all norm-107 elements lie in an asserted seed orbit. A binary equation is an intersection of the norm surface with the plane c=0, so returning to that plane is an additional exact condition.',
'Compute one unit action on the displayed norm element.',
'The element −1−3z corresponds to (−1,−3,0). Multiplication by ε=(1,−1,−1) gives (−13,−2,4), using z³=−4. Its norm remains 107 by multiplicativity, but its z² coefficient is four, so it is no longer a solution of the original two-variable equation.',
'The unit identities are verified, while the binary-plane return problem remains explicit.',
'The historical statement that (−1,3) is the only integer solution is not independently established here. A 3,631-candidate slab or modular period is not a global bound unless its reduction theorem and all excluded exponent classes are supplied and replayed.',
'Produce a complete certificate consisting of norm-orbit representatives, a proved fundamental-domain reduction, an explicit bound on unit exponents satisfying c=0, and exact evaluation of every residual candidate; then the claimed uniqueness can be independently checked.',
[('Mathlib: algebra norm as a determinant','https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RingTheory/Norm/Basic.html')])
